      "id": 1,
      "text": "Double Springs began in 1861 as a stage station on the Carson City‑Aurora road, serving travelers across Nevada.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://southwestexplorers.com/double-springs-nevada/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The station earned the nickname Round Tent Ranch, or Spragues, after a notorious stop on the road to Esmeralda.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://openroadguide.com/marker/double-springs",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Olds Toll Road linked Double Springs to Fairview, the headquarters of a famed horse‑thief network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://openroadguide.com/marker/double-springs",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Washoe and Paiute peoples once held spring and fall round dances here to celebrate pine‑nut harvests.",
